{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>With a rod.</b> Rambam wrote in chapter 2 of the Laws of a Murderer, and this is his language: 'It appears to me that this is specifically with a rod, but with a sword or a knife or a stone or a fist and the like, and they assessed him [as likely to die] — even if he died after a year, he [the master] is executed on his account.' End quote. And it is difficult for me: if he did not kill him with an instrument for which they assessed him as liable to execution, then why, if he does not survive a day, is he liable? For even regarding another person who is not his slave, he is exempt even if he does not survive a day. Perhaps he is speaking of the other assessments as well — that is, even where they assessed him as likely to die at the site of the wound and the other assessments. And it appears to me that Scripture's saying <b>with a rod</b> is to say that if his actions prove that his intent was to discipline him with a rod of rebuke — this is why HaShem granted him a one-day grace period, etc. — but if his actions prove that he intended to kill him, for example that he thrust a knife into his stomach and the like — this declares that he was not disciplining him but killing him, and [the slave] died even after a year, he is liable on his account."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:31:55.135783960Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}